WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:04.130
Speaker 1: Today we're going to be speaking
with Bob Zeidman, who's the creator of the

00:00:04.130 --> 00:00:09.090
field of Software Forensics, the founder of
several high tech firms.

00:00:09.090 --> 00:00:15.210
Author of textbooks and screenplays, novels,
a high stakes poker poker player in Vegas,

00:00:15.210 --> 00:00:22.840
and also the guy we mentioned who I guess
we could say won the Prove Mike Wrong challenge,

00:00:22.840 --> 00:00:28.280
which was the challenge from Mypillow CEO
Mike Lindell, known to many of us simply as

00:00:28.280 --> 00:00:34.500
Pillow, almost like Madonna, where he offered
up $5 million to anyone that could prove that

00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:41.320
the data he claimed showed China interfered
with the 2020 presidential election was not

00:00:41.320 --> 00:00:42.410
proving that.

00:00:42.410 --> 00:00:47.449
Bob, you prove that essentially, and now you
are in the process of, if I understand, attempting

00:00:47.449 --> 00:00:51.660
to collect this $5 million prize, which a
court has said is due to you, if that's the

00:00:51.660 --> 00:00:52.750
latest I've read.

00:00:52.750 --> 00:00:55.780
Speaker 4: Yes, David, thanks for having me
on the show.

00:00:55.780 --> 00:00:56.780
And that's correct.

00:00:56.780 --> 00:01:00.930
Technically, it was an arbitration, but it
was binding arbitration.

00:01:00.930 --> 00:01:02.539
Three arbitrators made that decision.

00:01:02.539 --> 00:01:06.930
You know, and one thing I think sometimes
isn't always clear, I'll just say this, is

00:01:06.930 --> 00:01:12.540
that, you know, the contest was not to prove
that there was no fraud, but it was actually

00:01:12.540 --> 00:01:17.100
broader than that, that the data didn't even
have anything to do with the 2020 election.

00:01:17.100 --> 00:01:21.980
Speaker 1: And, you know, I've interviewed
Mike Pillow a number of times, and he always

00:01:21.980 --> 00:01:27.300
says, listen, I'll send you the data and then
you and of course, I don't know what I would

00:01:27.300 --> 00:01:32.040
do with it because I'm not a cyber guy, as
he says, but he never sends the data.

00:01:32.040 --> 00:01:37.420
The first question is, where did you actually
get the data that was meant to be analyzed?

00:01:37.420 --> 00:01:40.640
Speaker 4: So he provided it indirectly.

00:01:40.640 --> 00:01:45.680
He had a team of people who gave it to a bunch
of experts.

00:01:45.680 --> 00:01:47.650
He invited a bunch of experts.

00:01:47.650 --> 00:01:52.430
Supposedly, we were all vetted so we couldn't
come to this symposium that he held about

00:01:52.430 --> 00:01:56.380
almost two years ago in Sioux Falls, South
Dakota.

00:01:56.380 --> 00:01:59.439
You couldn't come unless you're vetted as
an expert.

00:01:59.439 --> 00:02:01.340
And then he gave us the data.

00:02:01.340 --> 00:02:10.129
But at the arbitration, there were a lot of
excuses for why I well, why it wasn't evident

00:02:10.129 --> 00:02:11.730
that this was election data.

00:02:11.730 --> 00:02:12.989
Let me put it that way.

00:02:12.989 --> 00:02:18.150
I mean, he said it was election data, but
there are a lot of excuses that he didn't

00:02:18.150 --> 00:02:22.450
give us the whole data because of national
security issues, because he was afraid of

00:02:22.450 --> 00:02:27.370
hacking, because he thought that China or
somebody was threatening him, that it was

00:02:27.370 --> 00:02:29.590
encrypted, decoded, modified.

00:02:29.590 --> 00:02:38.660
Speaker 1: So but these these these claims,
as silly as they are, they do seem to go to

00:02:38.660 --> 00:02:41.420
Lindell really does believe this stuff.

00:02:41.420 --> 00:02:45.019
This isn't a joke for him because this is
something people often ask me since I've spoken

00:02:45.019 --> 00:02:48.700
to him a couple of times, Do I think he's
kidding or do I think he believes this stuff?

00:02:48.700 --> 00:02:50.080
He does seem to believe this stuff.

00:02:50.080 --> 00:02:52.800
Speaker 4: Yeah, So that was a big question.

00:02:52.800 --> 00:02:54.550
All the experts had that question.

00:02:54.550 --> 00:02:59.580
I can say we, as far as I know, unanimously
decided this was not election data.

00:02:59.580 --> 00:03:04.860
I was the one person who figured out what
it actually was and wrote up a detailed report

00:03:04.860 --> 00:03:06.129
and submitted it.

00:03:06.129 --> 00:03:10.900
But, you know, the question was, was Mike
bamboozled or was he bamboozling people?

00:03:10.900 --> 00:03:13.379
Yes, but he seems to be a true believer.

00:03:13.379 --> 00:03:19.620
But the only thing is any rational person
at this point would know the data is fake.

00:03:19.620 --> 00:03:26.140
But Mike has basically fired or insulted or
ostracized anyone who's come to him, including

00:03:26.140 --> 00:03:28.250
people in his team, have said that is fake
data.

00:03:28.250 --> 00:03:33.280
Speaker 1: So the data that was provided,
what was it?

00:03:33.280 --> 00:03:34.370
What was it data about?

00:03:34.370 --> 00:03:38.250
What was the the general area that it pointed
to?

00:03:38.250 --> 00:03:41.380
Speaker 4: Well, there were a number of files
that we got.

00:03:41.380 --> 00:03:46.110
I got five files the first day and or seven
files the first day.

00:03:46.110 --> 00:03:48.170
I forget now and four files the second day.

00:03:48.170 --> 00:03:53.739
It wasn't a whole lot of files, but one of
the, you know, some were PDFs showing generic

00:03:53.739 --> 00:03:56.610
voting machine processes.

00:03:56.610 --> 00:04:00.870
But the the more interesting ones is we got
a bunch of files that contain what are called

00:04:00.870 --> 00:04:03.989
hex data, but it was viewable hex data.

00:04:03.989 --> 00:04:05.711
And so I translated it.

00:04:05.711 --> 00:04:08.780
I got the idea to translate it to actual binary
data.

00:04:08.780 --> 00:04:13.180
And the way I would describe that is if you've
got a bunch of files that had written out

00:04:13.180 --> 00:04:21.470
in English language words 1254 61 and then
you translated those to the actual numbers,

00:04:21.470 --> 00:04:23.520
you know that that's equivalent to what I
did.

00:04:23.520 --> 00:04:28.240
Then I took those files and I noticed that
they were old fashioned because I've been

00:04:28.240 --> 00:04:32.370
in this business for a while, old fashioned
word processing documents.

00:04:32.370 --> 00:04:35.500
So I put them in a word processor, a microsoft
word.

00:04:35.500 --> 00:04:42.009
And lo and behold, what came up were one file
was a table, thousands of numbers that were

00:04:42.009 --> 00:04:43.009
just numbers.

00:04:43.009 --> 00:04:46.370
I mean, just a table of numbers that didn't
mean anything.

00:04:46.370 --> 00:04:50.690
And the other files were thousands of pages,
in one case of gibberish, as if someone had

00:04:50.690 --> 00:04:56.030
just sat there for hours typing, typing, typing,
then reverse the process I just did to make

00:04:56.030 --> 00:04:58.130
it look like sophisticated computer data.

00:04:58.130 --> 00:04:59.800
Speaker 1: So some of that.

00:04:59.800 --> 00:05:03.320
There's not even really a reason to think
it relates to elections at all.

00:05:03.320 --> 00:05:04.930
Never mind an American election.

00:05:04.930 --> 00:05:06.130
Speaker 4: Right.

00:05:06.130 --> 00:05:07.759
No, it definitely didn't.

00:05:07.759 --> 00:05:12.870
Although again, at arbitration, which was
fascinating, is different members of either

00:05:12.870 --> 00:05:17.320
Lindell and different members of Lindell's
team would come up with excuses like the gibberish

00:05:17.320 --> 00:05:23.669
was really encrypted data that they had purposely
encrypted or China had encrypted or, you know,

00:05:23.669 --> 00:05:27.910
depending on who was talking, that I wasn't
smart enough to see it.

00:05:27.910 --> 00:05:31.669
But the problem was they never they never
demonstrated what it really was.

00:05:31.669 --> 00:05:35.210
They said, oh, it's just sophisticated encrypted
data.

00:05:35.210 --> 00:05:39.259
And their excuse was, well, we don't have
to tell you what it is, because Zeidman it

00:05:39.259 --> 00:05:40.930
was his job to do it.

00:05:40.930 --> 00:05:45.900
If if he wanted to claim the $5 million and
the arbitrators, basically if you read their

00:05:45.900 --> 00:05:50.660
decision, they said even Lindell's team couldn't
demonstrate what this was other than gibberish.

00:05:50.660 --> 00:05:55.830
Speaker 1: I'm curious whether as as news
broke of your involvement in this and the

00:05:55.830 --> 00:06:01.830
arbitration decision about the 5 million,
did did you start getting threats from people

00:06:01.830 --> 00:06:04.340
or angry messages?

00:06:04.340 --> 00:06:08.620
Because it seems hard to do anything these
days without someone being absolutely furious.

00:06:08.620 --> 00:06:12.000
Did the any of the believers try to contact
you or threaten you?

00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:14.410
Speaker 4: So that's an interesting question.

00:06:14.410 --> 00:06:19.440
So first of all, you know, I'm a I'm a Republican
conservative.

00:06:19.440 --> 00:06:23.800
I've studied people who were not so happy
with me in the past couple of years.

00:06:23.800 --> 00:06:28.729
I do a lot of writing on political and social
issues, suddenly were friendly and telling

00:06:28.729 --> 00:06:30.590
me what a great job I did.

00:06:30.590 --> 00:06:35.090
But I have to say, even the conservatives,
the vast majority of them were thankful.

00:06:35.090 --> 00:06:39.250
Some of them believe that Lindell is hurting
Trump and hurting the Republicans.

00:06:39.250 --> 00:06:41.759
And I can tell you about that in a minute
if you want.

00:06:41.759 --> 00:06:46.590
But the other thing I'll say is I've gotten
like two or three threatening emails or Twitter

00:06:46.590 --> 00:06:47.590
messages.

00:06:47.590 --> 00:06:49.090
Nothing that I take seriously.

00:06:49.090 --> 00:06:50.620
Mostly people say they hate me.

00:06:50.620 --> 00:06:51.620
I'm a traitor.

00:06:51.620 --> 00:06:52.620
Sure.

00:06:52.620 --> 00:06:58.160
But but I went to a conservative conference
very with some very, you know, strong conservatives

00:06:58.160 --> 00:07:02.060
and even people who advise Trump.

00:07:02.060 --> 00:07:08.960
And at first I didn't want to mention what
I'd done, but it came out and the vast majority

00:07:08.960 --> 00:07:12.150
of them shook my hand and thanked me for what
I'd done.

00:07:12.150 --> 00:07:14.650
And a few of them started questioning, Why
did you do this?

00:07:14.650 --> 00:07:16.250
And I read this and I heard Lindell.

00:07:16.250 --> 00:07:20.699
And when I talked to them, by the end of the
conference, without exception, all of them

00:07:20.699 --> 00:07:21.699
thanked me.

00:07:21.699 --> 00:07:22.819
Speaker 1: Interesting.

00:07:22.819 --> 00:07:27.849
Now, you you mentioned that you're conservative.

00:07:27.849 --> 00:07:29.600
You voted for Trump twice, right?

00:07:29.600 --> 00:07:30.960
Speaker 4: Yeah.

00:07:30.960 --> 00:07:32.180
Yeah, I did.

00:07:32.180 --> 00:07:37.750
I can tell you that the first time I you know,
without going into detail, I did not like

00:07:37.750 --> 00:07:38.750
Trump.

00:07:38.750 --> 00:07:40.810
I followed his business practices.

00:07:40.810 --> 00:07:44.500
And I've had some problems, you know, on a
very, very tiny scale.

00:07:44.500 --> 00:07:45.919
I've done business with his businesses.

00:07:45.919 --> 00:07:52.440
And I didn't I didn't find his practices ethical,
but I thought it was but I really didn't trust

00:07:52.440 --> 00:07:53.830
Hillary Clinton.

00:07:53.830 --> 00:07:58.830
And I thought the Republicans would keep an
eye on Trump and the Democrats would not keep

00:07:58.830 --> 00:08:00.120
tabs on Hillary.

00:08:00.120 --> 00:08:01.120
Hmm.

00:08:01.120 --> 00:08:04.629
Speaker 1: Did you find that that's the way
it played out?

00:08:04.629 --> 00:08:10.639
Speaker 4: You know, it's is difficult to
say so because when Trump ran, there was a

00:08:10.639 --> 00:08:13.830
lot of Republican opposition to him.

00:08:13.830 --> 00:08:16.190
And of course there were the Never Trumpers.

00:08:16.190 --> 00:08:21.680
But with Hillary, the Democratic Party really
universally supported her as what I saw.

00:08:21.680 --> 00:08:27.960
Now, you know, Trump has basically made this
effort to take control of the Republican Party,

00:08:27.960 --> 00:08:33.580
which I don't like, because I think it's all
about him and not about ideology anymore.

00:08:33.580 --> 00:08:40.880
So I voted for him again reluctantly the second
time, but I'm hoping to have a third option,

00:08:40.880 --> 00:08:44.660
neither Republican or Democrat if it's Trump
v Biden.

00:08:44.660 --> 00:08:46.120
I really don't like either candidate.

00:08:46.120 --> 00:08:48.180
So I'm hoping they'll be a third option.

00:08:48.180 --> 00:08:49.990
Speaker 1: That's interesting.

00:08:49.990 --> 00:08:54.130
So have would you say that you've suffered
under the first two years of Biden in a way

00:08:54.130 --> 00:08:57.460
that makes you think, I've really got to get
someone else in there?

00:08:57.460 --> 00:08:59.550
Speaker 4: You know, I do.

00:08:59.550 --> 00:09:04.820
I think that Biden, you know, Biden was always
a middle of the road.

00:09:04.820 --> 00:09:08.589
Person, you know, politically until he ran
for president this time.

00:09:08.589 --> 00:09:09.589
Hmm.

00:09:09.589 --> 00:09:17.339
And it just seems like a lot of Far-Left policies
are being implemented or at least being given,

00:09:17.339 --> 00:09:19.810
you know, the okay from the White House.

00:09:19.810 --> 00:09:20.810
Speaker 1: Really?

00:09:20.810 --> 00:09:22.140
Now, that's interesting to me.

00:09:22.140 --> 00:09:26.529
This is super because I would love for Biden
to be far left.

00:09:26.529 --> 00:09:28.860
I don't see him as being that way.

00:09:28.860 --> 00:09:33.670
I'm curious what would be a policy that you
see as far left that was implemented?

00:09:33.670 --> 00:09:38.110
Speaker 4: Well, what I mostly see is that,
well, first of all, I don't like the spending

00:09:38.110 --> 00:09:40.820
bills that he's implemented.

00:09:40.820 --> 00:09:45.070
So I'm a member of an organization called
No Labels, which brings together Republicans

00:09:45.070 --> 00:09:46.070
and Democrats.

00:09:46.070 --> 00:09:47.070
I'm familiar with it.

00:09:47.070 --> 00:09:48.070
Okay.

00:09:48.070 --> 00:09:53.450
And so the Problem Solvers Caucus of No Labels
in Congress helped pass the first budget bill,

00:09:53.450 --> 00:09:57.410
which was in December of 2022, I believe.

00:09:57.410 --> 00:10:03.070
And then but then Biden passed his other one,
which was a lot more spending on programs

00:10:03.070 --> 00:10:04.070
I don't like.

00:10:04.070 --> 00:10:06.610
Speaker 1: Are you talking about the Inflation
Reduction Act here?

00:10:06.610 --> 00:10:07.610
Speaker 4: Yes.

00:10:07.610 --> 00:10:08.610
Yes.

00:10:08.610 --> 00:10:09.610
Speaker 1: Okay.

00:10:09.610 --> 00:10:10.610
Speaker 4: So, I mean, I don't like that.

00:10:10.610 --> 00:10:14.950
I think we're we're really you know, we've
gone way over budget.

00:10:14.950 --> 00:10:18.420
You know, we're going to break the debt ceiling,
obviously.

00:10:18.420 --> 00:10:20.800
I really don't like all that government spending.

00:10:20.800 --> 00:10:22.899
But also, although you know me, you know.

00:10:22.899 --> 00:10:26.709
Well, first of all, I think there are a lot
of things I think to pull out in Afghanistan

00:10:26.709 --> 00:10:28.110
was horrific.

00:10:28.110 --> 00:10:34.350
We lost more people in that pull out than
we had, I think, in the past year in Afghanistan.

00:10:34.350 --> 00:10:38.270
I don't like enabling the Taliban in Afghanistan.

00:10:38.270 --> 00:10:39.930
I don't like the border policy.

00:10:39.930 --> 00:10:44.540
We've had more illegal immigration than we've
had, I think, in American history.

00:10:44.540 --> 00:10:46.040
Speaker 1: How do you know that?

00:10:46.040 --> 00:10:51.320
Speaker 4: You know, I can't quote sources
offhand, but I have researched it.

00:10:51.320 --> 00:10:54.769
So one thing I do is I really try to look
into it.

00:10:54.769 --> 00:10:58.450
I publish articles and I really try to do
the research to find these numbers before

00:10:58.450 --> 00:11:00.410
I talk about them.

00:11:00.410 --> 00:11:01.410
Yeah.

00:11:01.410 --> 00:11:03.220
Speaker 1: Well, you know, I mean, I'm not
trying to play gotcha.

00:11:03.220 --> 00:11:05.650
And really, I mean, this is just interesting
to me, really.

00:11:05.650 --> 00:11:08.040
You're here to talk about the mike pillow
stuff.

00:11:08.040 --> 00:11:13.480
But what's interesting to me is clearly you're
a smart guy and you're technologically inclined

00:11:13.480 --> 00:11:18.410
and you sort of understand how we figure out
whether something is true.

00:11:18.410 --> 00:11:26.100
But like when you say the Biden spending,
the rate at which the debt has grown, slowed

00:11:26.100 --> 00:11:28.820
down under Biden, it's still growing, right?

00:11:28.820 --> 00:11:30.910
We're talking about the difference between
the debt and deficit.

00:11:30.910 --> 00:11:34.579
Of course, the debt is still growing under
Biden, but a little more slowly than under

00:11:34.579 --> 00:11:35.579
Trump.

00:11:35.579 --> 00:11:39.180
You'd think a guy like you who clearly is
so fact oriented would say, hey, you know

00:11:39.180 --> 00:11:43.740
what, I don't like it, but it is better than
what we were seeing under Trump.

00:11:43.740 --> 00:11:44.740
Is that fair?

00:11:44.740 --> 00:11:48.720
Speaker 4: Well, you know, I've heard those
arguments before.

00:11:48.720 --> 00:11:53.149
And the problem is even Republicans have have
been bad on spending.

00:11:53.149 --> 00:11:54.149
Sure.

00:11:54.149 --> 00:11:55.240
That's an issue for me.

00:11:55.240 --> 00:12:01.250
And I think that politicians in general, the
best they can they've ever done is reduce

00:12:01.250 --> 00:12:03.060
the increase in the deficit.

00:12:03.060 --> 00:12:06.390
But it's still a dangerously increasing deficit
in my mind.

00:12:06.390 --> 00:12:07.390
Sure.

00:12:07.390 --> 00:12:13.269
I mean, I'd love to see politicians get in
and Congress and in the presidency that could

00:12:13.269 --> 00:12:14.690
actually cut programs.

00:12:14.690 --> 00:12:19.310
Look, I'm willing I'm a Republican who's willing
to cut spending in the military because although

00:12:19.310 --> 00:12:23.620
I think our military needs to be strong, it
also needs to spend efficiently.

00:12:23.620 --> 00:12:24.620
Sure.

00:12:24.620 --> 00:12:25.900
And just throwing more money at it.

00:12:25.900 --> 00:12:27.490
And this is a problem that I see.

00:12:27.490 --> 00:12:30.510
Like, you know, I'll criticize the Republicans
here.

00:12:30.510 --> 00:12:34.540
The Republicans have to go into an election
if they want to win, saying we're going to

00:12:34.540 --> 00:12:36.589
spend more money on the military.

00:12:36.589 --> 00:12:42.269
But in reality, I'd love to see them say we're
going to spend less money on the military

00:12:42.269 --> 00:12:44.240
for more efficient operations.

00:12:44.240 --> 00:12:47.600
I mean, and by the way, I could I could have
another show.

00:12:47.600 --> 00:12:52.800
I sued the U.S. government for fraud because
I uncovered fraud.

00:12:52.800 --> 00:12:53.889
It's a longer story, but.

00:12:53.889 --> 00:12:56.300
Speaker 1: Well, but that's I mean, that's
interesting.

00:12:56.300 --> 00:13:00.560
Without getting into that, your view on the
military is not one I expect to hear from

00:13:00.560 --> 00:13:04.010
any presidential Republican candidate, even
if they believe it, because it wouldn't be

00:13:04.010 --> 00:13:05.840
politically viable to say it.

00:13:05.840 --> 00:13:13.399
Question for you, Are you sort of a little
bit concerned about the degree to which the,

00:13:13.399 --> 00:13:17.070
quote, Anti-woke platform is taking over the
Republican Party?

00:13:17.070 --> 00:13:18.070
Or do you like that?

00:13:18.070 --> 00:13:20.019
Speaker 4: Well, I like that.

00:13:20.019 --> 00:13:21.019
Oh, you do?

00:13:21.019 --> 00:13:22.019
I know.

00:13:22.019 --> 00:13:23.019
I know we disagree on that.

00:13:23.019 --> 00:13:29.459
But you know something I can tell you, I've
written about this, so and I'll put it in

00:13:29.459 --> 00:13:30.899
my personal perspective.

00:13:30.899 --> 00:13:36.820
I'm Jewish and I even criticize Jewish groups
that are just I understand them.

00:13:36.820 --> 00:13:42.079
They want they want to eliminate anti-Semitism,
which is a great cause.

00:13:42.079 --> 00:13:47.199
But in doing so, there's this race to be the
world's greatest or the country's greatest

00:13:47.199 --> 00:13:48.199
victim.

00:13:48.199 --> 00:13:55.339
And I think this victimhood mentality is increasing
the racial conflict in the country.

00:13:55.339 --> 00:13:58.940
And I don't know how to get away from it because
each group, I think, looks at another group

00:13:58.940 --> 00:14:02.310
